From: cactus@bb.hks.net (L. Todd Masco)
Date: 15 Nov 1994 22:46:20 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-214] Quote of the day

In article <199411120056.AA03439@panix.com>,
Gregory Sullivan <sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu> wrote:
>"L. Todd Masco" <cactus@bb.hks.net> quotes:
>>"Unless there are slaves to do the ugly, horrible, uninteresting work,
>> culture and contemplation become almost impossible. Human
>> slavery is wrong, insecure, and demoralizing. On mechanical
>> slavery, on the slavery of the machine, the future of the world
>> depends."
>>
>>	Oscar Wilde, "The Soul of Man Under Socialism," 1895
>
>This is an interesting quote.  Is there any indication of the level of
>sophistication Wilde envisioned as being achievable by machines? Did
>he express a belief in what we would now call artificial intelligence
>or was he talking about relatively simple machines.   gfs

Neither... he seemed to have automated factories in mind with no
specifics.  

When I first posted the above quote I hadn't read the essay, having run
across it on the Web.  I finally yanked out my "Complete Works of Oscar
Wilde" today and read the essay.

It's an odd little beast, using "Socialism" is a slightly different
way than we do today.  His view in the article is probably best summed
up as "anarchosocialist":  He advocates the abolition of private property
in the service of "Individualism," postulating that people will spend all
their time Thinking Deep Thoughts if all production is automated and
property done away with.  This is not a socialism of "the people," though:
see the quote below.

It's got some really great quotes in it.  Here's another:

"There are three kinds of despots.  There is the despot who tyrannises
 over the body.  There is the despot who tyrannises over the soul.  There
 is the despot tyrannises over the body and soul alike.  The first is
 called the Prince.  The second is called the Pope.  The third is called
 the People...All despots bribe.  The People bribe and brutalise."

And:

"It is to be noted that Individualism does not come to the man with any
 sickly cant about duty, which merely means doing what other people want
 because they want it; or any hideous cant about self-sacrifice, which is
 merely a survival of savage mutilation.  In fact, it does not come to a
 man with any claims upon him at all.  It comes naturally and inevitably
 out of man.  It is the point to which all development tends.  It is the
 differentiation to which all organisms grow.  It is the perfection that
 is inherent in every mode of life, and towards which every mode of life
 quickens.  And so Individualism exercises no compulsion over man.  On
 the contrary, it says that he should suffer no compulsion to be
 exercised over him"

Like most philosophers, he makes no concrete proposals as to how to "get
there from here," and makes no comments on how to reconcile "no compulsion
to be exercised over him" with the mechanics of abolishing property.  I
think he might have expected everybody to say, "Oh!  We won't have
property anymore!" or something.

Still, it's got some great rants & quotes.
