Well, Jeremy Rifkin and Paul Ehrlich are demonstrably nuts.  Rifkin keeps
rattling on about the "Fourth Law of Thermodynamics" (no such beastie) and
Ehrlich keeps back-dating his hypotheses to cover up the fact that his
Population Bomb is a dud.

As for the Earth being in danger -- you been believin' too much of what you
see on TV, boy.  Get thyself Simonized (to borrow Mistress Romana's lovely
term) -- Julian Simon has done an excellent job of debunking most of the
alarmists in his books _The Ultimate Resource_, _Population Matters_, and
_The Resourceful Earth_.

To quote a favorite DC Comics character from my childhood:  WISE UP,
GULLIBLE FOOLS!  YOU'RE BEING MANIPULATED!

>Questions: Answer yes or no.
>
>1.  Natural ecosystems and other natural life forms (e.g., not cows)
>are good things worth preserving, even if there is no material benefit
>and significant material loss to us from doing so.

Mu.  You clearly haven't heard of E-Prime yet.  How about "I consider
natural ecosystems etc. worth my time, efforts, and resources to preserve,
even if there is no material benefit and significant material loss to me in
doing so."  You're free to do what you want, but don't try to sign me up.
My personal take on the matter (YMMV) is that (1) the ecosystem is a
sufficiently complex machine and sufficiently necessary for our
(short-term, and therefore long-term) survival that we ought not to go
throwing away pieces of it wholesale until we know what they do -- but (2)
I haven't seen sufficient evidence to convince me that we're in danger of
throwing away enough pieces to worry about (see infra).

>2.  Natural ecosystems are being disrupted, species eradicated, and
>the environment polluted at rates beyond optimal.

Nope, sorry.  The evidence in favor of this hypothesis is weak, sporadic,
over-reported, and over-extrapolated.  (1) Atmospheric CO2 levels have
failed to rise anywhere near as fast as trumpeted by doomsayers.  _Science
News_ recently reported that most of the missing carbon seems to be showing
up in the form of increased growth of deep-rooted range grasses in
prairie/savannah biomes worldwide.

Furthermore, if you were Gaia, would you be _pro_ or _con_ an increase in
CO2?  Okay, you're a planet-girdling life-form.  Your metabolism runs on
CO2, water, and sunlight (i.e., photosynthesis).  Plants take up CO2 and
turn it into sugars (of varying levels of complexity).  Animals are
supposed to turn around and put the CO2 back into the economy again, but
their population is limited to the rate of production of sugars -- which is
in turn limited by the available CO2.  Catch-22.

Then, in the middle of the night last night (well, over the last 20,000
years or so -- a blink of an eye to a ponderous Earth-goddess like
yourself), along came this wondrous animal that can actually release CO2 by
_faster_ means than mere respiration!!  (All those cookfires that drove the
Ice Age out of Europe, for example.)  Lovely!  Your ecosystems fizz with
more CO2, enabling you to grow more plants, produce more sugars, feed more
animals, generate more species.  What a bargain!  (And now some of these
animals are talking about _reproducing_ you elsewhere in space!  Such a
deal!)

(2) The CO2 that _is_ ending up in the atmosphere is not leading to the
global warming prophesied.  Mean Northern Hemisphere temperatures over the
past twenty years have been fluctuating around a more-or-less even keel,
not demonstrating the canonical rise predicted.

>3.  There is a danger that if we do not reduce such disruption,
>eradication, and pollution soon, it will be "too late" (feel free
>to give your own interpretation of "too late").

We're overdue for the next Ice Age by about 2,000 years, according to the
geological record -- what, oh what, could be holding it back?  "Button up
your overcoat/When the wind blows free..."  I hope it's not that disruption
and pollution you're trying to call a halt to...

>4.  The Earth will soon have too many humans.

No way.  P. J. O'Rourke, in his newest book _All The Trouble In The World_,
points out that the entire population of the Earth could live in Texas and
Oklahoma at the population density of present-day San Francisco (calculate
how much land that leaves left over for agriculture -- about 100%, I'd
say).  OK, now take a plane flight over the western U.S., a jaunt along the
Trans-Siberian Railroad, and a camel ride across Western Australia.  Note
that none of these areas are inherently uninhabitable; they just aren't
conveniently accessible yet.  (Minnesota is as cold and harsh as Siberia,
but you don't see Prince -- excuse me, *@(&^# or whatever his name is --
wearing a parka and a furry hat in his videos nowdays.)  People crowd
together for economic and social reasons, but there's PLENTY of room left
on this planet.

>5.  The Earth has too many humans already.

As World Entertainment War said, "The problem is not overpopulation -- It's
overpopulation by the wrong people."  What we need is sufficient right of
contract to make it worthwhile and economical to educate the people we've
already got -- that is, to allow people to sell a fraction of their future
labor in exchange for the schooling necessary to become economically
useful.  (What?  You say that every child in America already owes $17,000
dollars in public debt that went, in part, to pay for their schooling?
Yeah, but I'm talking about doing it _voluntarily_, not at gunpoint.)

>6.  Population should increase until the Earth contains as many humans
>as it can support.

Ever put too many rats in a cage, Phil?  Humans aren't as social as some
folks would have you believe; as a species, we don't take to crowding well.
(That's part of the reason why cities have such higher crime rates than
rural areas.)  Don't worry, Phil, we'll be tearing each other apart with
our fingernails and teeth before getting anywhere _near_ Standing Room
Only.  (See?  Dynamic Optimism at work!)  The Four Horsemen, as Heinlein
said, are always saddled up and ready to ride...

>7.  Everyone should be content to live in a city.  Country living is a
>luxury which takes too many resources per individual.

I can't tell if you're advocating this position or making fun of it.  Have
you ever lived in the country?  What makes you think it's more expensive
than city living?  How much cheaper is venison you shot yourself, compared
to Safeway beef that's had six unionized butchers and the USDA's fat hands
all over it?  How much cheaper is it to build the kind of house _you_ want,
instead of the kind overspecified by building codes and overtaxed by the
city fathers so the homeless can sleep in a decent public library?  How
much cheaper is it to have enough room to grow your own vegetables (of
whatever variety you prefer) with no distribution costs, no warehousing, no
"Lucky, Lucky, dare to compare" advertising costs?  How much cheaper is it
to pay $5 a month in automobile insurance, instead of $50,  because there's
NO DAMN TRAFFIC?  How much cheaper is it to be able to be able to buy food
in bulk (or put up your own preserves), because your house can be big
enough for a big freezer and pantry?  I could go on -- I grew up on a farm
in Nebraska -- but I think I've made my point.  The country is heaven, my
boy.  It's cheap livin', with a lot less wear and tear on the nerves.

>8.  The Earth is ours, and we have no obligations to other species.

What obligations would they recognize to us?  Does "one who recognizes
obligations to those who do not recognize reciprocal obligations" sound
like a good definition of "sucker" to you?

>9.  Intellect is the greatest virtue, and creatures with greater intellect
>should eventually replace all creatures of lesser intellect.

"Replace", no.  Think how bored a world-class intellect like (say) Marilyn
vos Savant would be, ringing up your purchases at Thrifty Drug.  George F.
Will might be entertaining on _Meet the Press_, but would you want him as a
household pet?  Every niche in the ecosystem will be filled, whether it's a
biological or an economic ecosystem.  I prefer, "Intellect is the ultimate
resource, and actions that increase the total intellect available tend to
increase the availability of all other resources."

>Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu

