>From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
>Date: Tue, 15 Nov 1994 15:24:47 -0500
>Subject: [#94-11-205] Bush robot, Environment (Re: HUMOR: tarot)
>
>>>6.  Population should increase until the Earth contains as many humans
>>>as it can support.
>>
>>How many people exist should be a result of individual decisions to have
>>children and to continue to live.
>
>In an ideal world with no resource restrictions, yes.  I'm talking
>about the real world.

But, but -- the real world has no resource restrictions!!  Go outside and
look up on a clear night.  See any limits?  Look at some landmark you know
to be a mile away.  Now visualize, standing on the ground between you and
it, an asteroid a mile in diameter.  Calculate how much nickel, iron, ices,
etc., such an asteroid would contain.

What, that's too long-term for you?  Okay.  Find a 1940's Philco TV set and
a Sony Watchman.  Weigh them, and take a ratio.  Say the Philco weighs 50
pounds and the Watchman weighs one.  That gives us a rate of mass reduction
of .02 per fifty years for TVs.  (The Sony gets more channels, with better
sound, in color, and you don't get a hernia hauling it around, but ignore
those values... assume a TV is a TV.)  Where did that other 98% of the mass
go?  I don't know -- but you can use it to make something else besides
TV's!!  Bucky Fuller called this ephemeralization -- doing more with less.
As our population and standard of living increase, the amount of mass
needed to maintain the standard of living decreases as technology improves.
Computers are the canonical example, but everything from electric razors
to bicycles is smaller, lighter, and more capable than it was decades ago,
and just as cheap (or cheaper), relative to wages.  (The taxation hidden in
inflation due to deficit spending -- the government gets more spending
